          "distinct": true,
          "id": "00089e04120be3aa511544b02f4f05be2a617400",
          "message": "Add the SelectPruneAfter light tier: never-rejecting chains skip the filter driver\n\nSelect.PruneAfter and PruneAfter.Select composed onto the Where\ndriver, paying promotion path state for chains that can never carry\nSkipNode -- the same disease the prune-over-prune fix patched\npairwise, now solved structurally. Representations follow the\nrelabeling gradient:\n\n- ISelectTreenumerable widens into ISelectPruneAfterTreenumerable\n  (: ISelectWhereTreenumerable): the light tier for chains of\n  projections and prune-afters. Its two methods are the type-enforced\n  tier boundary -- Compose(selector) returns a bare value,\n  ComposePruneAfter(predicate) a bool; neither can smuggle SkipNode.\n  The tier never relabels, so positional lambdas always compose\n  across it.\n- Implementers: the Select wrapper (projections stay lightest, a\n  prune-after promotes to the middle tier), the PruneAfter wrapper\n  (prune-over-prune keeps merging on the bespoke driver; a projection\n  promotes), and the new SelectPruneAfterTreenumerable -- mixed\n  chains on a dimension-agnostic passthrough driver: no promotion\n  machinery, no path state, positions forwarded unchanged, result\n  strategies merged into the pull after each scheduling visit.\n- A rejecting operator converts to the SelectWhere representation\n  via the inherited general Compose.\n\nMeasured: Select over the canonical pruned triangle now costs +0.22KB\nover traversing the tree bare (58.81 vs 58.59 KB DFT, 226.61 vs\n226.39 KB BFT) -- the tier is construction-cost only. The battery's\nprojection/prune-only chains certify the routing; four new pins hold\nthe representation choices. All 24,326 tests green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
