          "id": "5c520d2c5481de766e5709c444341222f2eda5f6",
          "message": "Generate the narrow lattice twins: CompositeToNarrow, the dimension phase\n\nThe twelve narrow (single-dimension) SelectWhere-lattice files were\nhand-mirrored from their composite-width sources — every wrapper edit had to\nbe made three times. The dimension axis, like the color axis, cannot be\nabstracted by C# generics (the successor type constructor is what varies), so\nit gets the color axis's remedy: transcription. CompositeToNarrow narrows the\ncore contract and every return type, renames the lattice types through a\nclosed dictionary (exact-match, so driver near-miss names pass through), and\ndrops the other dimension's acquisition method.\n\nGeneratorManifest gains NarrowGeneratorEntry and twelve narrow rows; Program\nruns the narrow phase before async->sync, whose manifest now lists the narrow\n.g.cs outputs as inputs — one composite-width async file fans out to five\ngenerated twins (narrow async x2, sync x3), and a wrapper implementation edit\nis made in exactly one place. GeneratedTwinDriftTests gains the narrow-phase\nguard; the two tests together prove the whole chain is fresh. The operator\noverloads' probe tables stay hand-written (their narrow and composite forms\ndiffer genuinely). No benchmark rows: the generated twins are behaviorally\nidentical to the hand-written files they replace. 24,337 green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
